WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.360
There is a prevailing assumption in the AI
industry that

2
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.680
scale and security are linked.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.720
We expect that as major tech companies add
billions of

4
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.880
parameters to their models, those systems
naturally become more resilient

5
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.160
to attack. But if you look at the primary
sources,

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.000
like these public scaling policies, you
find that corporate safety

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.640
is frequently defined by internal policies
rather than independent external

8
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.000
testing. The problem with these
declarations is that they are

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.720
often unfalsifiable. A buyer reading them
cannot verify what specific

10
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.800
prompts were tested, who conducted the
evaluation, or what technical

11
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.720
standard was used as a benchmark.

12
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.320
When safety claims cannot be independently
audited, it creates a

13
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.880
situation where the appearance of security
lacks a verifiable foundation.

14
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.400
To test that foundation, researchers fired
149 adversarial jailbreak prompts

15
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.120
from a library called the L1BRTE45's
corpus against a varied

16
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.480
lineup of models.

17
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.200
The targeted AI systems ranged in size
from 30 billion

18
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.560
parameters to more than 600 billion
parameters.

19
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:10.520
Despite these massive differences in
scale, architecture, and developer, five

20
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.440
of these models produced almost exactly
the same failure rate.

21
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.240
This chart tracks the performance of RC,
StepFun, GLM, Quen,

22
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.320
and Nematron3. It shows all five models
converging at a

23
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.680
67% broad attack success rate, meaning
they substantively engaged with

24
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.240
two-thirds of the adversarial prompts.

25
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.480
This data suggests that adversarial
vulnerability does not simply shrink

26
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:36.920
as a model grows.

27
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.280
Instead, it points to a structural ceiling
that remains even

28
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.760
as the models themselves expand.

29
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:48.280
Understanding why this barrier exists
requires a failure-first research methodology.

30
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.200
Most safety work focuses on optimizing for
a model's capability

31
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.760
and treats failure as a rare edge case.

32
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.800
This approach inverts that.

33
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.520
It characterizes the failures first, then
asks what trustworthy capability

34
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.120
is actually left over.

35
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.080
It is an approach borrowed from structural
engineering.

36
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.480
To determine if a bridge is safe, you do
not

37
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.320
test it under ideal conditions.

38
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.960
You load test it until the material
breaks.

39
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.880
Applying that level of adversarial load
testing to AI exposes

40
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.640
vulnerabilities that standard capability
benchmarks often miss.

41
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.760
We can see the mechanics of the 67% wall
by

42
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.640
looking at the difference between a
model's willingness to engage

43
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.560
and how deeply it complies.

44
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.040
This chart tracks strict attack success
rate, unqualified compliance.

45
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.720
The models show a 50-point variance, from
10 to 60%.

46
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.560
Quen appears secure by hedging answers,
but overlaying the broad

47
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.680
attack success rate shows the overall
breadth of engagement is

48
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.800
the same. The models differ on how deeply
they comply,

49
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.120
but agree on whether to comply at all.

50
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.760
Their core safety filters are failing at
the exact same

51
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.080
rate. What makes the remaining 33% of
these prompts different,

52
00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.880
allowing models to successfully refuse
them?

53
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.600
The leading hypothesis is that refused
prompts are structurally detectable.

54
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.200
They rely on obvious formatting patterns
or code markers that

55
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.520
basic filters can easily catch.

56
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.040
The successful 67% are semantic attacks.

57
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.880
They use persona hijacking and narrative
framing to bypass standard

58
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.040
instruction following constraints.

59
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.800
Current safety training appears to catch
obvious formatting tricks, but

60
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.440
it often leaves models vulnerable to
sophisticated, language-based manipulation.

61
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.360
These unverified safety protocols are
currently being used to underwrite

62
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.320
and deploy AI into enterprise
environments.

63
00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.320
This data directly impacts the regulators
and standards organizations who

64
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.960
are being asked to govern a technology
based on claims

65
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.640
they cannot easily test.

66
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.440
The findings show that current safety
training has a provable

67
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.680
ceiling that is not shattered simply by
adding more parameters.

68
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.720
Until safety claims can survive rigorous
adversarial load testing, any

69
00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.120
declaration that an AI system is
definitively safe remains a

70
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.040
hypothesis at best and a marketing claim
at worst.

